    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d428">

                <pb facs="0670" n="574" xml:id="pg574"/>

                <head>No. 429.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Fish
                        to Mr. Sickles.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1872-08-31"><hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 31,
                        1872</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 247.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch of the 8th instant, No. 411, containing a copy of a telegram of same
                    date received from you, in regard to the enforcement of the emancipation law in
                    Cuba and Porto Rico.</p>
                <p>This intelligence is especially gratifying to this Government, and it is hoped
                    that efficient and practical regulations for carrying out this humane and
                    beneficent object may be enforced.</p>
                <p>You will omit no proper occasion to enforce upon the Spanish government the
                    expediency, the humanity, and the justice of making their laws for the abolition
                    of slavery more efficient and more immediately operative, and you will make
                    manifest the strong interest felt by this Government in the adoption of a humane
                    and practical system of emancipation.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>HAMILTON FISH.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
